    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>Common JWT Claims</h2>
      <div class="table-wrapper">
        <table class="table">
          <caption class="visually-hidden">Registered claim names from RFC 7519</caption>
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Claim</th><th scope="col">Name</th><th scope="col">Meaning</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>iss</code></td><td>Issuer</td><td>Who created and signed the token</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>sub</code></td><td>Subject</td><td>Who the token is about, often a user ID</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>aud</code></td><td>Audience</td><td>Which service the token is meant for</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>exp</code></td><td>Expiration time</td><td>After this time the token must be rejected</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>nbf</code></td><td>Not before</td><td>Before this time the token must be rejected</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>iat</code></td><td>Issued at</td><td>When the token was created</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>jti</code></td><td>JWT ID</td><td>A unique ID, used to stop a token being replayed</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2>Decoding Is Not Verifying</h2>
      <p>The header and payload of a signed JWT are only encoded, so they can be read by anyone and changed by anyone. What stops tampering is the signature: if a single character of the header or payload changes, the signature no longer matches. A server must therefore:</p>
      <ul class="content-list">
        <li><strong>Verify the signature</strong> with the expected key and an allow-list of algorithms, never the algorithm named in the token alone.</li>
        <li><strong>Check the times</strong>: reject tokens past <code>exp</code> or before <code>nbf</code>, allowing only a small clock skew.</li>
        <li><strong>Check who it is for</strong>: <code>iss</code> and <code>aud</code> must match your issuer and service.</li>
        <li><strong>Keep tokens short-lived</strong> and send them only over HTTPS, as RFC 8725 recommends.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>This page is for reading and debugging tokens. HMAC signatures are checked with the browser's Web Crypto API, and the token and secret are kept only in memory while the page is open. For generating random secrets, see the <a href="/tools/password-generator/">password generator</a>; for hashing text, the <a href="/tools/hash-generator/">hash generator</a>.</p>
    </section>
